.TH "GIT-WHOAMI" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
git\-whoami \- show the name, email and signing key git will use here
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
git whoami [options]
git-whoami [options]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Git takes the author of every commit from its config, and that config comes in layers. The system file, your global \fB~/.gitconfig\fR, files it includes with \fBincludeIf\fR, and the repository's own \fB.git/config\fR can all set \fBuser.email\fR, and the last one wins. The first sign of a mix\-up is a work commit pushed under your personal email, and by then it is in the history.
.PP
\fBgit whoami\fR shows what git will use in the folder you are in, and which file set each value.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fR and \fBemail\fR, the author of your next commit
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsigning\fR, the key and format, and whether commits are signed without \fB\-S\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBremote\fR, one line per remote, with its URL
.PP
Run it in a new clone before the first commit, or after you change \fBincludeIf\fR rules.
.PP
It only reads. It works outside a repository too, and then shows the global settings and no remotes.
.PP
It is a script named \fBgit\-whoami\fR in toolbelt's \fBbin\fR folder. Git runs any program named \fBgit\-NAME\fR on your PATH as \fBgit NAME\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
One line, \fBName <email>\fR, for scripts and prompts.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show each git command before it runs.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH WHERE EACH VALUE COMES FROM
.PP
The third column names the file, from \fBgit config \-\-show\-origin \-\-show\-scope\fR.
.TP
\fB~/.gitconfig\fR
Your global config. \fB~/.config/git/config\fR is the other place git reads it from.
.TP
\fB.git/config, this repo only\fR
The repository's own config. It beats the global one here.
.TP
\fB.git/config.worktree, this worktree only\fR
A worktree's own config, when \fBextensions.worktreeConfig\fR is on.
.TP
\fB/etc/gitconfig, every user\fR
The system config.
.TP
\fB~/.gitconfig\-work\fR or another file
A file pulled in by \fBinclude\fR or \fBincludeIf\fR.
.TP
\fBgit \-c or GIT_CONFIG_* variables\fR
A value set on the command line or through the \fBGIT_CONFIG_COUNT\fR variables.
.TP
\fBthe GIT_AUTHOR_NAME variable\fR
The environment. It beats every config file.
.PP
Git uses \fBGIT_AUTHOR_NAME\fR and \fBGIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL\fR over \fBuser.name\fR and \fBuser.email\fR when they are set, and falls back to the \fBEMAIL\fR variable when no config sets an email. \fBgit whoami\fR follows the same order.
.SH THE SIGNING LINE
.TP
\fBnone, commits are not signed\fR
No signing key and \fBcommit.gpgsign\fR is off.
.TP
\fBssh ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub, commits are signed\fR
\fBgpg.format\fR is \fBssh\fR, the key is set and \fBcommit.gpgsign\fR is on.
.TP
\fBopenpgp ABCD1234, commits are not signed unless you pass \-S\fR
A key is set, but git signs only with \fBgit commit \-S\fR.
.TP
\fBopenpgp, the key that matches your email\fR
Signing is on and no key is set, so gpg picks the key for your email.
.PP
Turn signing on for every commit with \fBgit config \-\-global commit.gpgsign true\fR.
.SH HELP AND THE MAN PAGE
.PP
\fBgit whoami \-h\fR and \fBgit\-whoami \-\-help\fR print the help. \fBgit whoami \-\-help\fR makes git open the man page \fBgit\-whoami(1)\fR, which toolbelt installs from this document.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
None. \fBgit whoami\fR runs \fBgit config \-\-get\fR for each key and \fBgit remote\fR, and \fB\-v\fR prints them.
.SH NEEDS
.PP
git 2.30 or newer, from the \fBgit\fR package on every distro. \fB\-\-show\-scope\fR needs git 2.26.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS In a work repository
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ git whoami
name     Khadirullah                   ~/.gitconfig
email    khadirullah@work.example.com  .git/config, this repo only
signing  ssh ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub, commits are signed
remote   origin git@github.com:khadirullah/toolbelt.git
.fi
.RE
.PP
The email comes from this repository's own config, so commits here use the work address.
.SS Outside any repository
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ cd ~
$ git whoami
name     Khadirullah              ~/.gitconfig
email    khadirullah@example.com  ~/.gitconfig
signing  none, commits are not signed
.fi
.RE
.SS One line for a script
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ git whoami -q
Khadirullah <khadirullah@work.example.com>
.fi
.RE
.SS The environment wins
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="Build Bot" git whoami
name     Build Bot                the GIT_AUTHOR_NAME variable
email    khadirullah@example.com  ~/.gitconfig
signing  none, commits are not signed
.fi
.RE
.SS Nothing set yet
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ git whoami
name     not set. Set it with: git config --global user.name "Your Name"
email    not set. Set it with: git config --global user.email you@example.com
signing  none, commits are not signed
git-whoami: git refuses to commit until user.name and user.email are set
$ echo $?
1
.fi
.RE
.SS See the git commands
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ git whoami -v
+ git config --show-scope --show-origin --get user.name
+ git config --show-scope --show-origin --get user.email
name     Khadirullah                   ~/.gitconfig
email    khadirullah@work.example.com  .git/config, this repo only
+ git config --show-scope --show-origin --get gpg.format
+ git config --show-scope --show-origin --get user.signingkey
+ git config --show-scope --show-origin --get commit.gpgsign
signing  ssh ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub, commits are signed
+ git remote
remote   origin git@github.com:khadirullah/toolbelt.git
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
The email is my personal one in a work repository : Set it for this repository with \fBgit config user.email you@work.example.com\fR. For every repository under one folder, add an \fBincludeIf\fR block to \fB~/.gitconfig\fR, such as \fB[includeIf "gitdir:~/work/"]\fR with \fBpath = ~/.gitconfig\-work\fR, and put the work email in that file.
.PP
Commits already went out with the wrong email : \fBgit whoami\fR only shows what comes next. Fixing old commits means rewriting them, which is only safe before they are pushed. \fBgit commit \-\-amend \-\-reset\-author\fR fixes the last one.
.PP
\fBcommits are signed\fR but GitHub shows them as unverified : GitHub needs the same public key under Settings, SSH and GPG keys, added as a signing key, and the email on the commit must be one of your verified emails.
.PP
\fBgit\-whoami: user.name or user.email is not set, git refuses to commit\fR : \fB\-q\fR found no name or email. Set both with \fBgit config \-\-global\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Name and email are both set.
.TP
1
The name or the email is not set, so git refuses to commit.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as an argument.
.TP
3
git is not installed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBgit\-sync\fR, \fBgit\-undo\fR, \fBgit\-config(1)\fR
